# Self-Adjoint Operators & Quadratic Forms {#sec:intro}

Let $A \in \Sym$ be a real symmetric $n \times n$ matrix ($A = A^\mathsf{T}$).
See [[linear-algebra|Linear algebra notes]] and the full LaTeX proof in [[spectral-theorem.tex]].
With the standard inner product $\inner{x}{y} = \sum_{i=1}^n x_i y_i$ on $\R^n$, symmetry is equivalent to the self-adjoint identity:

$$
\inner{Ax}{y} = \inner{x}{Ay} \quad \forall x, y \in \R^n.
$$ {#eq:selfadj}

Associated with $A$ is the **Rayleigh quotient** $R_A : \R^n \setminus \{0\} \to \R$:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:rayleigh}
R_A(x) = \frac{\inner{Ax}{x}}{\norm{x}^2}.
\end{equation}

::: definition {#def:rayleigh title="Extremal Rayleigh values"}
For $A \in \Sym$, define $\lambda_{\min}(A) = \min_{x \ne 0} R_A(x)$ and $\lambda_{\max}(A) = \max_{x \ne 0} R_A(x)$ via @eq:rayleigh.
:::

::: theorem {#thm:spectral title="Real Spectral Theorem"}
Every real symmetric matrix $A \in \Sym$ admits an orthogonal decomposition
$A = Q \Lambda Q^\mathsf{T}$ where $Q^\mathsf{T} Q = I_n$ and $\Lambda = \diag(\lambda_1, \dots, \lambda_n) \in \R^{n\times n}$.
:::

::: proof
By @eq:selfadj, every eigenvalue of $A$ is real and eigenvectors corresponding to distinct eigenvalues are orthogonal. Proceeding by induction on $n$ on the invariant subspace $v_1^\perp$ completes the decomposition.
:::

